cartridge "SNAKE PIT/Michael L. Hall/2026"

' SNAKE PIT
' A ColecoVision game based on "Snake Pit", COMPUTE!'s Gazette December
' 1987 (Commodore 64 machine language, disk files SNAKE PIT.BOOT and SNAKE
' PIT; the program names no author). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI
' made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: one or two snakes in a walled pit, five minutes on the clock.
' Mice run out of six holes in the walls; steer your snake onto them. A
' snake never stops; running into a wall, a snake (your own too) ends the
' round, and the pit is set up again while the clock keeps running.
'
' From the original (orig/snakepit.dis, read from its code and played in
' jsc64): one or two players (two joysticks), five speeds (the C64 waited
' 65, 49, 33, 17 or 1 delay loops a step, speed 3 the default); a snake
' starts 8 long (one player: in the middle heading right; two players: one
' above heading right, one below heading left); nothing moves until the
' stick is pushed (both sticks with two players); every step the four mice
' move first, then the snakes (player 2 before player 1); a mouse picks one
' of four directions at random, stays put if that is straight back or not
' free, and an eaten mouse comes back out of one of the six holes (1 in 6
' each) when that hole is free, facing into the pit; a mouse eaten = 30
' points and the snake one longer; a snake that crashes turns yellow, the
' other player gets 20 (nothing is lost with one player), a pause of about
' a second, then the pit, the snakes and the mice are set up again; the
' clock (5:00, shown counting down) runs all the time and ends the game;
' scores of six digits. The stick: up is read first, then right, down,
' left (a diagonal means up or right, as on the C64).
'
' Liberties: the pit is 26 x 19 cells inside the walls (the C64's was
' 38 x 23), holes and start places scaled to it; the steps take 10, 8, 6,
' 4 or 3 frames for speeds 1-5, so that the snake crosses the pit in about
' the C64's time (measured in jsc64: a step every 110 ms at speed 1, the
' delay loop 1286 cycles a count plus about 25 ms of work, so about 110,
' 89, 68, 47 and 26 ms: 4.2, 3.4, 2.6, 1.8 and 1.0 s across 38 cells;
' here 4.3, 3.5, 2.6, 1.7 and 1.3 s across 26); heads and mice glide a pixel at a time between
' cells; the stick is remembered until the next step (the C64 read it once
' a step); pulling straight back is ignored (on the C64 it was an instant
' crash into the snake's own neck); the snake's body is drawn in joined
' pieces; a dead snake turns white (yellow would vanish on the dirt);
' a piece of each snake marks its score; the game end names the winner with two players; one title page
' chooses players and speed (the C64 had two pages); no colour flashing.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (with one player either controller): the stick
' steers. Player 2 uses controller 2. On the title, up and down choose a
' line, left and right change it, a button starts (or shows HOW TO PLAY or
' the credits).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off,
' display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after; the title
' tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

